WEBVTT

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:02.480
The showdown that will split apart the heavens,

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:03.730
Yakitate!! 9!

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:05.900
Here are the contestants!

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:11.990
What’s this?

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:14.860
There’s only one contestant from St. Pierre’s side.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.660
I’ve seen him somewhere before.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:21.870
Isn’t he Tsubodzuka Takumi, a member of CMAP?

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:23.370
Oh, that’s right!

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:25.540
Hey, wait!

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:28.920
They’re having an entertainer as our opponent, and there’s just one of them, too...

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:30.420
Are they making fun of us?

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:31.880
Hey, you bald baker...

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:33.260
Be quiet.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:34.260
Who you calling bald?!

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.390
I’m not bald anymore!

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:36.640
Shut it!

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:41.930
Just concentrate on Super Taste Judge
Kuroyanagi Ryou’s explanation of the game’s rules.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:43.900
Fine.

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:48.440
Take a look at the nine yakitate panels that are posted on this screen.

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:52.740
Beneath each of these panels is the name of a city.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:57.200
You will be having a bread battle that
utilizes the selected city’s specialty products.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.290
The winner will obtain that panel,

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:02.950
and in the end, the side with the most panels will win.

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:04.870
However, this is television!

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:08.090
There are various activities under several panels.

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:09.460
But, let’s leave that aside.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:10.970
You’re leaving that aside?

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.470
So, let’s open the very first panel!

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:18.930
Ooma

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:21.140
Daikan? Ookan?

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:22.100
Ookan?

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.480
Isn’t it Ooma?

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:25.520
That’s right. Ooma.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:28.690
This is Ooma, a city in Aomori prefecture, Shimokita district.

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:31.570
Map Television, open!

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:33.240
Your battle will be here,

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.320
a small port-city at the northernmost point of the island of Honshuu.

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:45.210
There’s English bread, German bread, and French bread,

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:47.130
but Japan’s bread, <i>Japan</i>, does not exist.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:49.420
In that case, there’s no choice but to create it.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:52.900
This story is a serious, biographical ballad of a boy who possesses Solar Hands,

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:57.840
Azuma Kazuma, who will create a Japanese bread made by and for the Japanese people,

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.900
which can be presented to the world proudly.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:12.570
I want this voice to reach you, even though it’s still a tiny song.

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:18.740
That’s because I have the feeling that something’s gonna start.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:33.880
As I was cowering, muffling my voice, the tears that I shed

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:42.430
probably helped me notice that

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:48.350
I’ve almost forgotten how to believe.

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:57.200
The future that I painted (I will fulfill it)

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:02.620
Looking up to the clear skies,

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:09.710
I begin to run once again. Someday, I want to dye

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:15.550
that white sky with my colors.

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:22.550
I’m going to fly as far as possible, shining like a marble,

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:26.890
unfolding these wings of mine.

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:35.940
The bread battle in special localities!!

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:38.370
In the end, he’s a pop idol!

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:44.240
Oh! He’s on the front page of this magazine!

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:46.450
Aren’t these TRUMP guys amazing?

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:48.200
It’s CMAP, you idiot.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:49.790
CMAP...

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:52.210
Cooking Meal Assemble People.

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:57.300
It’s an acronym, meaning
“people assembled to cook and taste.”

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:00.300
It’s Japan’s supreme group of idol cooks.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:05.720
For our first match, we have someone really strong turn up, huh?

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:09.350
But... Being too strong isn’t a good thing, either.

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:10.310
Don’t you think so?

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.520
You really fail as a person.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:14.850
Yeah, a failure of a human being.

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:16.190
But, Kanmuri...

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:19.030
What’s the specialty product of
this Ooma city that we’re going to?

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:22.030
If we don’t even know that, we
can’t possibly start to make the bread.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:25.530
Super tuna is the most famous.

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:28.080
Tuna you buy from a supermarket?

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:29.410
How can that be?!

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:31.540
But what is that, Kanmuri-kun?

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:34.440
Ooma is an extremely famous fishing harbor

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.090
that produces large quantities of superior quality seafood.

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:40.460
And amongst them, the tuna,
which is widely known as “super tuna,”

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:44.050
can fetch as much as
20,000 yen per kilogram at Tsukiji.
Note: Tsukiji (<b>築地) is a fish market in Tokyo.</b>

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:45.590
What was that?!

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:48.890
So, 200 kilos of tuna would go for... umm...

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:49.810
4 million yen?!

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:53.270
However, no matter how delicious a bread we make,

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:58.860
if it’s so expensive that neither the locals
nor the tourists can afford it, we can’t win.

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:01.690
This is quite a difficult problem.

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:04.860
The northernmost city of Honshuu – Ooma

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:06.740
We’re finally here.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:07.950
Uh...

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:10.950
These are our lodgings?!

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:12.330
Shinisou
Shinisou...

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:14.580
Yeah, it sure does look like it’d die at any moment.
Shinisou

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:15.830
Kanmuri-kun...
Shinisou

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:17.710
It can’t be helped.

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:21.710
At the very least, <i>we</i> must be thrifty,
or all of our money will disappear quickly.

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:22.690
But...

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:25.510
According to Mr. Kirisaki’s conditions,

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:30.680
the televising and production fees of
the program are all our responsibility.

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:33.430
You mean our enemy’s lodging expenses and stuff are all our...

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:34.640
Yep.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:36.730
The enemy team’s lodging expenses...

00:05:36.730 --> 00:05:39.650
The lodging expenses of Kuroyanagi-senpai and the program staff...

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:41.440
The transportation expenses, the food and drink expenses...

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:44.950
All those bills are sent to us.

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:48.910
The one thing that St. Pierre
will be paying for on their own

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:51.660
is the money spent on making their own bread.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:53.790
H-How terrible.

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:56.830
Why did we accept those conditions?

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:01.340
Didn’t we all get carried away and accept them?!

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:05.840
Okay, so I guess we must all save, right, Kanmuri-kun?

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:09.340
There’s no need to hold back.

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:12.100
Please eat all you like.

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:13.350
This is good. Just as expected!

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:16.020
The fat is still attached! It’s great!

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:18.100
Chef, super toro please,

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:19.690
50 more pieces.

00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:20.570
Right-o.

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:23.860
After all, they’re going to be paying for this.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:26.120
I’ll eat enough to kill them off.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:28.410
What’s wrong, Tsubodzuka-kun?

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:30.530
Eat up.

00:06:30.530 --> 00:06:32.280
This tuna gets a Negative.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:33.120
Huh?

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:37.580
I’d like a more aggressive,
Positive feel to it.

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:40.670
I-I wonder, is this kid quite all right...?

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:43.290
With such a popular idol, we
should be able to rake in the viewers...

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:46.210
Moreover, Kirisaki gave him
his seal of approval as a cook...

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:48.430
That’s why we requested his participation...

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:48.880
Chef.

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:51.720
This is a little too terrible.

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:53.350
Can I have you change with me?

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:54.560
What was that?!

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:56.220
Hey, Tsubodzuka-kun!

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:59.350
I’m sorry. Please don’t worry about it.

00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:00.770
You damn brat.

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:03.360
Don’t be causing unnecessary problems.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:05.110
Hey, youngster.

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:07.490
By “change,” do you mean you
want to exchange places with me?

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:09.900
Sounds like fun. Why don’t we see what you’ve got.

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:10.820
Oh, no!

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:20.920
Here you go. Your order of 50 pieces of ootoro.

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:23.420
What?!

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:25.750
I-I don’t believe it.

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:27.420
I’ve been doing this for 30 years.

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:29.420
To think that he can make it so much faster than me...?!

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:31.930
Chef... This is the <i>real</i> toro.

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:33.850
What do you mean by “real toro”?

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:37.180
Whether or not you make it fast,
the toppings are the same.

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:40.650
So, it will obviously taste the same.

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:50.530
And it’s caught. Splash!

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:52.030
Delicious!

00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:55.030
It tastes completely different from my toro!

00:07:55.030 --> 00:07:58.160
It feels like snow melting in my mouth.

00:07:58.160 --> 00:07:59.540
How can this be?

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:01.040
You cut with the grain, right?

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:02.040
Yeah.

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:06.000
Normally, toro is cut against the muscles,

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:07.460
that is, you cut against the grain.

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:10.720
But, by cutting along the grain,

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:14.890
Tsubodzuka separated out each muscle, creating a toro that melts like snow.

00:08:14.890 --> 00:08:16.810
What a guy...

00:08:16.810 --> 00:08:20.560
That’s how you made them so fast, is it?

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:21.890
That’s not all.

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:22.520
What was that?

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:25.270
Try eating the piece that’s on the extreme right.

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:28.730
This is the actual sinew of the muscle, right?

00:08:28.730 --> 00:08:31.610
You can’t feed this to people!

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:33.820
Please file your claims after having tried it.

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:39.710
It couldn’t possibly be good!

00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:42.620
It’s delicious!

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:46.130
The more I chew, the more
the flavor deepens and seeps out!

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:50.420
The sinew of the muscle has the tuna’s deep flavor.

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:52.130
Of course it’s good to eat.

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:56.840
Also, it’s been pressed firmly,
making it good for chewing.

00:08:58.930 --> 00:09:00.390
Listen up, Chef.

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:03.640
While it’s just called “toro,”
there are many ways of making it!

00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:05.690
You may very well have been doing this for 30 years,

00:09:05.690 --> 00:09:09.070
but if you’re just doing it the same as everyone else, you’re no different from a robot!

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:11.940
I-I give up.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:16.370
Looks like his skills are a sure thing.

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:18.370
I’m relieved.

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:21.910
Tsubodzuka-kun, can I order 30 more pieces?

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:25.420
Mr. Yamaka, that’s really eating a bit too much.

00:09:25.420 --> 00:09:27.370
No, there’s no more toro.

00:09:27.370 --> 00:09:31.210
If I use too much in a day, I won’t last until August.

00:09:31.210 --> 00:09:33.340
Huh? What do you mean?

00:09:33.340 --> 00:09:36.090
Can’t you just fish more tuna from here?

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:41.640
We begin to catch tuna in the summer.
The colder it gets, the fattier the tuna are.

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:45.020
We only fish in Ooma from August until January.

00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:46.640
But, Ooma is known for its tuna.

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:46.850
Hotel Utopia

00:09:46.850 --> 00:09:47.310
But, Ooma is known for its tuna.

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:53.650
Ooma’s hotels and sushi shops store the tuna in freezers and get it out from February to April.

00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:54.780
Eh?

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:58.160
So, what if the stuff in the freezer were to run out?

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:01.460
In that case, I’d go to a nearby hotel or some place that stores tuna

00:10:01.460 --> 00:10:04.250
and ask them to split some with me.

00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:07.000
And should the stock in the hotel run out too...?

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:09.210
In that case, we can’t do anything.

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:10.790
Is that so?

00:10:10.790 --> 00:10:12.590
I can use this.

00:10:14.590 --> 00:10:16.840
It looks like everybody’s here.

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:20.640
Then, let me once again
explain the details of the rules to you.

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:25.060
First, you must use a
specialty food of Ooma in your bread.

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:27.600
As long as at least one specialty food is included,

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:30.190
you can use any number of other ingredients in it.

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:34.280
But, if you use items from near
the city and within the prefecture,

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:36.030
it will gain you points.

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:41.030
The biggest objective of this battle is to
create a long-lasting bread for each area.

00:10:41.620 --> 00:10:43.120
By using the specialties of the area,

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:46.790
you must make a bread that will be
loved by both tourists and the locals.

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:49.000
The time limit is 3 days.

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:54.040
I can’t think of any way to use super tuna.

00:10:54.620 --> 00:10:57.510
For now, how about trying to get some tuna?

00:10:57.510 --> 00:10:58.840
That’s right.

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:00.560
Yeah, first let’s go and see.

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:01.720
What was that?!

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:02.930
There’s no tuna?!

00:11:02.930 --> 00:11:07.650
Yeah, we got nonstop orders for tuna since yesterday.

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:09.100
No way!

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:10.150
Here, too?!

00:11:10.150 --> 00:11:14.270
I’m very sorry, but we’re sold out on all the tuna here.

00:11:14.270 --> 00:11:16.440
I hate to say it, but since we don’t have any here...

00:11:16.440 --> 00:11:20.450
I would bet that you won’t find tuna anywhere in Ooma.

00:11:22.280 --> 00:11:23.780
What does this mean...?

00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:26.700
How can all of the super tuna in Ooma be sold out?

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:30.620
If would be safe to assume that
someone bought out the entire supply.

00:11:31.250 --> 00:11:33.430
Do you think that it’s a scheme of Yukino’s?!

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:40.590
This is just about the season when the frozen tuna is defrosted and offered to tourists.

00:11:40.590 --> 00:11:43.640
She must have set her eyes on
the fact that the supply is limited.

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:46.600
So, what are we supposed to do, then?

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:47.920
Say, Kanmuri...

00:11:48.860 --> 00:11:52.860
What are the months that most tourists come here?

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:57.740
W... Well, from around the end of April to around Golden Week in May.

00:11:58.110 --> 00:12:00.450
This is the northernmost point of Honshuu.

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:03.450
In the winter, it’s too cold and no tourists come.

00:12:03.450 --> 00:12:04.570
How about the summers then?

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:09.410
Most seafood, including tuna, caught in
the summer is watery and therefore bad.

00:12:09.410 --> 00:12:11.410
That’s why no one comes in the summer either.

00:12:12.920 --> 00:12:14.670
Then, let’s not use tuna.

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:16.880
Why not?!

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:19.380
No matter how superbly delicious Ooma’s tuna is...

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:21.340
it’s ultimately frozen stuff, right?

00:12:21.340 --> 00:12:24.390
But, when you travel,
wouldn’t you want to eat fresh food?

00:12:24.390 --> 00:12:26.360
Isn’t that what people would normally want?

00:12:27.470 --> 00:12:29.390
If we want to make a bread that would

00:12:29.390 --> 00:12:33.400
be popular among both tourists and
locals like Old Man Kuroyanagi wants,

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:35.650
then I don’t think this will do.

00:12:35.650 --> 00:12:37.570
I see...

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:39.570
But, then...

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:46.110
Well, lookie here... If it isn’t the folk from Pantasia...

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:47.070
Yukino!

00:12:53.960 --> 00:12:58.260
Eating super tuna sushi under the sky of the
northernmost point of Honshuu

00:12:58.260 --> 00:13:00.300
is really special...

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:01.800
What are you here for?!

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:06.050
I actually came here with the thought of sharing some with you...

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:06.760
Huh?

00:13:07.220 --> 00:13:08.800
Here you go.

00:13:10.810 --> 00:13:12.180
What’s this?

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:13.810
Tuna eyeballs.

00:13:14.890 --> 00:13:18.020
Tuna eyeballs have a lot of DHA in them,

00:13:18.020 --> 00:13:22.320
so I thought that if you sucked on them,
you might come up with a good idea.

00:13:23.650 --> 00:13:24.820
See ya, then!

00:13:24.820 --> 00:13:26.280
What’s with her?!

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:27.370
I’ve decided.

00:13:27.370 --> 00:13:28.580
Let’s go with that.

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:29.950
Oh, Kanmuri-chan...

00:13:29.950 --> 00:13:31.950
You’ve thought of something good?

00:13:31.950 --> 00:13:33.540
Was it due to the effect of the tuna eyeballs?

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:35.000
That’s not possible, is it?

00:13:36.250 --> 00:13:38.210
Isn’t this a fishery?

00:13:38.210 --> 00:13:39.920
Oh, sea urchins!

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:41.920
They’re alive!

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:45.130
Unlike tuna and other seafood
that are harvested in winter,

00:13:45.130 --> 00:13:48.300
sea urchin is harvested from March to June.

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:53.270
So it’s the perfect ingredient for
Golden Week, when most tourists visit.

00:13:53.270 --> 00:13:56.190
Ooma’s sea urchins are really high quality,

00:13:56.190 --> 00:13:58.730
and are many times cheaper than super tuna.

00:13:58.730 --> 00:14:02.690
Also, most sea urchins
exported to Tokyo and far-off places

00:14:02.690 --> 00:14:05.820
are preserved in pickeringite, a very bitter preservative.

00:14:05.820 --> 00:14:09.070
But if they’re cooked where they’re
caught, there isn’t a need for that.

00:14:09.070 --> 00:14:12.790
So, we should be able to cook urchin here,
bringing out its flavor to the maximum.

00:14:12.790 --> 00:14:14.910
All right, that’s great!

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:16.830
This is purple sea urchin, right?

00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:21.500
The roe of purple urchin is white.
The whiter the roe is, the sweeter it is.

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:24.170
And the roe of Ooma’s urchins looks really white!

00:14:24.170 --> 00:14:28.090
There’s purple and white sea urchin, Kanmuri-kun?

00:14:28.090 --> 00:14:29.890
You really don’t know?

00:14:29.890 --> 00:14:30.680
Nope.

00:14:31.180 --> 00:14:34.680
Typically, there are two varieties of sea urchin that are eaten.

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:36.980
Bafu urchin, harvested in winter,
has red roe.

00:14:36.980 --> 00:14:40.060
The roe of Ooma’s urchin, purple urchin, is white.

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:43.270
Why do you know so much about them, Azuma-kun?

00:14:43.270 --> 00:14:46.150
Well, our family grows rice, after all.

00:14:46.150 --> 00:14:48.030
So we also do business with sushi shops.

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:51.570
You really know a lot, Mio-chan.

00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:54.160
With this urchin, I can make it!

00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:56.950
Ooma <i>Japan</i>, the highest quality chawanmushi-bread!

00:14:56.950 --> 00:14:59.120
Ch-Chawanmushi-bread?

00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:01.480
Kawachi, I have a favor to ask of you.

00:15:02.090 --> 00:15:04.170
Would you make me some French bread?

00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:09.090
No! If Kawachi-san were to make it, we’d lose!

00:15:09.090 --> 00:15:10.550
That’s right, Azuma!

00:15:10.550 --> 00:15:12.260
What are you making me say, you ass!

00:15:12.260 --> 00:15:15.730
No, it has to be Kawachi who makes it.

00:15:16.220 --> 00:15:20.000
After all, that French bread must taste bad.

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:23.020
Taste bad, you say?

00:15:32.370 --> 00:15:34.200
What an amazing trailer!

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:39.080
This is a special trailer made for the cooking confrontations for the Yakitate!! 9 show.

00:15:39.080 --> 00:15:41.360
The interior contains cooking instruments

00:15:41.360 --> 00:15:44.080
and everything else you could possibly need to bake bread.

00:15:44.710 --> 00:15:47.210
The program has a lot of our matches on location.

00:15:47.210 --> 00:15:49.300
Therefore, something like this is absolutely essential.

00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:52.300
Umm... Could it be that this is also...?

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:54.890
Of course, it’s a part of the
production cost of the program.

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:56.810
So, it’s being paid for out of our wallets.

00:15:56.810 --> 00:15:58.680
By the way, one trailer is 50 million yen.

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:00.020
Five... Five... Five...?!

00:16:00.020 --> 00:16:03.980
By the way, both Pantasia
and St. Pierre have their own,

00:16:03.980 --> 00:16:04.780
so in total, there are two.

00:16:04.780 --> 00:16:06.860
100 million yen?!

00:16:06.860 --> 00:16:09.070
How can this be...?

00:16:09.070 --> 00:16:11.760
I’m making bad French bread that won’t be worth a single yen

00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:14.870
in a trailer worth 50 million yen...

00:16:14.870 --> 00:16:16.030
Would you look at that.

00:16:16.030 --> 00:16:18.370
It’s made pretty well.

00:16:18.370 --> 00:16:20.340
I’m surprised.

00:16:22.620 --> 00:16:26.460
How’s that? You understand my strength now...?

00:16:28.840 --> 00:16:30.130
What is it, Azuma?

00:16:30.130 --> 00:16:32.390
Are you touched that it’s done so well?

00:16:33.470 --> 00:16:35.800
No! This is not good! Not good!

00:16:35.800 --> 00:16:37.260
Wait... What is it?

00:16:37.260 --> 00:16:40.480
Your bread wasn’t this crunchy before.

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:44.020
It was just slightly sticky,
and was therefore kind of bad.

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:45.560
Sticky?

00:16:45.560 --> 00:16:48.270
That’s what I want!

00:16:49.030 --> 00:16:50.280
Huh?

00:16:50.940 --> 00:16:52.570
I don’t want the present Kawachi.

00:16:52.570 --> 00:16:54.660
Please go back to the way you used to be!

00:16:54.660 --> 00:16:56.240
Asking for something like that is...

00:16:56.240 --> 00:17:03.000
I made the slashes shallow, the loaf
shorter and the ends flatter like you asked.

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:06.000
What can I do to make it worse?

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:09.750
Short with flat ends?

00:17:09.750 --> 00:17:11.000
I see!

00:17:11.420 --> 00:17:14.760
Could it be that the chawanmushi-bread
that you’re aiming for is like this...

00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:16.720
And, like that...?

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:18.010
Yep.

00:17:18.010 --> 00:17:19.260
I was right!

00:17:19.260 --> 00:17:23.480
Kawachi-san, make the bread again, and this time add a little more water please.

00:17:23.480 --> 00:17:24.560
Excuse me?

00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:25.480
Understood?

00:17:25.480 --> 00:17:27.940
Just go back to the way you used to be.

00:17:27.940 --> 00:17:33.240
Then, you’ll be able to satisfy Azuma-kun by making a bad French bread.

00:17:34.150 --> 00:17:35.660
Well then, I’m off.

00:17:35.660 --> 00:17:37.860
Hey, where are you going?

00:17:37.860 --> 00:17:41.330
I’ve finally figured out what it is
that Azuma-san is aiming for.

00:17:41.330 --> 00:17:43.410
So, I’m going to obtain a secret weapon.

00:17:43.410 --> 00:17:45.910
A... A secret weapon?

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:50.340
Azuma-san!

00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:52.840
Oh, Tsukino! And Manager!

00:17:53.380 --> 00:17:55.430
It’s finally time for the first match, right?

00:17:55.430 --> 00:17:57.220
Are the preparations going well?

00:17:57.220 --> 00:17:58.720
They’re perfect!

00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:00.640
Kanmuri got me a secret weapon,

00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:03.220
and Kawachi has made me some bad French bread.

00:18:03.220 --> 00:18:05.730
B... Bad?

00:18:07.230 --> 00:18:08.810
Listen up.

00:18:08.810 --> 00:18:13.360
We will now begin the first
match of Yakitate!! 9, Ooma.

00:18:13.360 --> 00:18:13.820
Well then, let’s...

00:18:13.820 --> 00:18:14.860
Mr. Judge.
Well then, let’s...

00:18:15.280 --> 00:18:16.780
I have a request.

00:18:16.780 --> 00:18:17.490
What is it?

00:18:17.490 --> 00:18:19.870
I was wondering if I could use
my personal oven for this match.

00:18:19.870 --> 00:18:21.080
Would that be all right?

00:18:21.080 --> 00:18:22.080
What?

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:23.370
You’ve brought it with you?

00:18:23.370 --> 00:18:27.500
I don’t mind, but I won’t forgive you for interrupting me again.

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:28.370
Okay.

00:18:28.370 --> 00:18:29.830
His personal oven?

00:18:30.500 --> 00:18:31.670
Well then, once again...

00:18:31.670 --> 00:18:33.090
Hey!
Well then, once again...

00:18:33.460 --> 00:18:37.090
Can I also use my personal Ooma city garbage bag?

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:40.970
Whether it’s your personal Ooma item,
or your personal Aomori item,

00:18:40.970 --> 00:18:44.100
or your personal Tokyo bag,
just do as you like.

00:18:44.100 --> 00:18:45.730
I can’t do that.

00:18:45.730 --> 00:18:48.600
Since it’s a bread battle using the area’s specialty items,

00:18:48.600 --> 00:18:51.600
I want to use a local garbage bag too.

00:18:52.110 --> 00:18:54.320
Fine, whatever.

00:18:54.320 --> 00:18:55.740
Huh? Wait.

00:18:55.740 --> 00:18:56.610
A garbage bag?

00:18:57.260 --> 00:18:58.110
He okayed it.

00:18:59.070 --> 00:19:02.200
He’s planning something weird again, isn’t he?

00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:03.740
There isn’t anything else, is there?

00:19:03.740 --> 00:19:04.830
All right!

00:19:04.830 --> 00:19:06.540
Then, let’s start!

00:19:07.080 --> 00:19:09.580
Then, can you help out here, Kanmuri?

00:19:09.580 --> 00:19:12.250
Kawachi has to make bad French bread!

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:19.720
After that, just as Kanmuri asked, I made
French bread with more water in it.

00:19:19.720 --> 00:19:21.220
Oh, this is bad!

00:19:21.220 --> 00:19:22.680
This is what I wanted!

00:19:22.680 --> 00:19:25.930
This slight stickiness is what makes it your bread!

00:19:25.930 --> 00:19:28.930
What a messed-up compliment...

00:19:28.930 --> 00:19:30.060
Look here!

00:19:30.060 --> 00:19:32.810
When we first met,

00:19:32.810 --> 00:19:37.490
my bread may have been stickier than yours since I wasn’t born with Solar Hands.

00:19:37.490 --> 00:19:39.950
But now, I have Solar Gauntlets,

00:19:39.950 --> 00:19:42.820
and my skills as a baker have improved as well.

00:19:42.820 --> 00:19:46.280
So, it’s not easy for me to make bad bread.

00:19:46.280 --> 00:19:49.120
In order to get rid of the effect of the Solar Gauntlets,

00:19:49.120 --> 00:19:51.300
I had to cool down my muscles in iced wafer.

00:19:51.300 --> 00:19:53.040
The North Pole Hands!

00:19:53.040 --> 00:19:57.880
Also, while kneading the dough, instead of doing it lightly like you or Manager,

00:19:57.880 --> 00:20:03.300
I mashed up the flour and the removed the gas after fermentation by punching the dough.

00:20:03.300 --> 00:20:06.300
And then, this is the bread that was created.

00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:12.480
Somehow... Making bread while remembering my old self has made my nose leak.

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:17.180
I think I was a really terrible
baker when I first came to Pantasia.

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:20.400
What a nostalgic taste.

00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:22.110
He’s not listening to me...

00:20:22.110 --> 00:20:26.740
Well then, I’ll go select
some good urchins for tomorrow.

00:20:26.740 --> 00:20:29.950
I’m expecting the same terrible
bread in the match tomorrow as well.

00:20:29.950 --> 00:20:36.960
In the end, I’m being made to make bad French bread without knowing the reason why.

00:20:37.340 --> 00:20:39.380
Are we really all right?

00:20:39.380 --> 00:20:42.640
The enemy comes in with his own personal oven, and we have...

00:20:43.130 --> 00:20:46.730
bad French bread and an Ooma city garbage bag.

00:20:52.520 --> 00:20:55.060
As expected... CMAP is amazing, right?

00:20:55.060 --> 00:20:58.860
That man’s skills are pretty good.

00:20:58.860 --> 00:21:03.490
Right now, he’s making two types of dough. Do you know what types of dough those are?

00:21:03.490 --> 00:21:07.620
Uh... One is probably pie dough.

00:21:07.620 --> 00:21:09.990
That stiffness probably means that it’s pastry dough.

00:21:09.990 --> 00:21:11.160
Yeah.

00:21:11.160 --> 00:21:12.540
So, what about the other?

00:21:12.540 --> 00:21:14.040
Hmm...

00:21:14.040 --> 00:21:15.000
It’s choux dough.

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:15.830
Note Choux dough (pâte à choux): This is the basic dough used to make eclairs, profiteroles and cream puffs.

00:21:15.830 --> 00:21:18.840
By choux dough, do you mean choux creme dough?

00:21:18.840 --> 00:21:19.960
Yeah.

00:21:19.960 --> 00:21:21.340
This is bad, Tsukino.

00:21:21.340 --> 00:21:23.840
He’s really strong.

00:21:23.840 --> 00:21:27.980
Without a doubt, he’ll make a
pastry of the highest quality.

00:21:32.310 --> 00:21:34.140
How’s it looking, Kawachi?

00:21:34.140 --> 00:21:36.480
Yeah, it’s almost done.

00:21:36.480 --> 00:21:37.480
Is that so?

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:39.270
Hey, you guys.

00:21:39.600 --> 00:21:40.650
Tsubodzuka...

00:21:40.650 --> 00:21:43.820
Guys, that bread is way too Negative.

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:47.740
If you’re going to give up,
then just say so and give up.

00:21:48.120 --> 00:21:49.160
Huh?

00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:50.620
I don’t quite get you...

00:21:50.620 --> 00:21:53.370
Do you mean that if we’re
going to make that terrible a bread,

00:21:53.370 --> 00:21:55.710
then we should just accept our defeat?

00:21:55.710 --> 00:21:56.710
Yeah.

00:21:57.460 --> 00:22:00.460
This little piece of...

00:22:00.460 --> 00:22:04.460
But, he’s got a point...

00:22:05.970 --> 00:22:08.890
Hey Azuma, let’s stop this.

00:22:08.890 --> 00:22:13.270
The moment the super tuna ceased to be available, our loss was sealed.

00:22:13.270 --> 00:22:15.140
Making a bread like this is not going to help us win...

00:22:15.140 --> 00:22:17.190
Huh? What are you talking about?

00:22:17.190 --> 00:22:20.480
I wouldn’t use this crappy bread just like that.

00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:22.480
How harsh! But, what was that?!

00:22:22.480 --> 00:22:24.490
I take this bread...

00:22:24.490 --> 00:22:26.110
do this...

00:22:26.110 --> 00:22:28.960
and then it goes into the garbage bag for burnable trash!

00:24:05.210 --> 00:24:06.170
Hey!

00:24:06.170 --> 00:24:08.510
He put my French bread into the garbage bag!

00:24:08.510 --> 00:24:10.050
What are you doing, Azuma?!

00:24:10.050 --> 00:24:11.680
Please calm down,

00:24:11.680 --> 00:24:13.930
because there’s nothing more you can do, Kawachi-san.

00:24:13.930 --> 00:24:15.010
Hey!

00:24:15.010 --> 00:24:16.770
Next time, on Yakitate!! Japan,

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:17.350
Awaken!!

00:24:17.350 --> 00:24:18.770
Super Kuroyanagi!

00:24:18.770 --> 00:24:19.980
Be sure to watch.

00:24:19.980 --> 00:24:22.730
Yakitate!! Seed of knowledge.

00:24:22.730 --> 00:24:26.980
The specialty of Aomori, ichigoni, is a
Japanese-style soup with a lot of sea urchin in it.

00:24:26.980 --> 00:24:31.740
Apparently, since the urchins look like wild strawberries, it was named “ichigoni.”

00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:34.290
It’s not a dessert, right?
